@h1 Build your own renderer h1@

The ship renderers are just tree walks. Anyone can write another — to LaTeX,
RTF, JSON, Slack blocks, plain text, anything. A renderer is a pure recursive
walk over @code ExpandedDocument.children code@; @@@code @witlang/render-html code@@@
(~350 lines) and @@@code @witlang/render-markdown code@@@ are the reference
implementations to mirror.

@h2 The kinds you actually handle h2@

After @code expand code@ the tree is narrow. You handle: @code paragraph code@,
@code text code@, @code italic code@, @code bold code@, @code nodeUse code@, and a
stray @code record code@ or @code collection code@ (from a definition body that
collapsed to a literal). Everything else — @code nodeDef code@,
@code dataDef code@, @code reference code@, @code interpolation code@,
@code bodySlot code@, @code if code@, @code each code@ — should be @strong gone. strong@
Treat a survivor as an error or warning. @code comment code@,
@code scriptBlock code@, and @code scriptCall code@ may appear but are normally
dropped.

@h2 The minimal renderer h2@

A render-to-plain-text you can run today. It is the whole pattern:

@@@pre
import type { ExpandedDocument } from '@witlang/runtime';
import { isReservedNodeName, RESERVED_OPAQUE } from '@witlang/runtime';

function render(node: any): string {
  switch (node.kind) {
    case 'paragraph':  return node.children.map(render).join('') + '\n\n';
    case 'text':       return node.value;
    case 'italic':     return '*' + node.children.map(render).join('') + '*';
    case 'bold':       return '**' + node.children.map(render).join('') + '**';
    case 'nodeUse':    return renderNodeUse(node);
    case 'record':     return node.fields.map((f: any) =>
                          `${f.key}: ${renderValue(f.value)}`).join('\n');
    case 'collection': return node.items.map(renderValue).join('\n');
    default:           return ''; // if/each/defs should be gone by now
  }
}

function renderNodeUse(use: any): string {
  const body = (use.body ?? []).map(render).join('');
  if (use.name === RESERVED_OPAQUE) return dispatchByType(use, body); // @node
  if (isReservedNodeName(use.name)) return body;    // core vocab: your mapping
  return body;                                      // user def: body was inlined
}

function getParam(use: any, name: string): string | undefined {
  return use.params.find((p: any) => p.name === name)?.value;
}
pre@@@

@h2 Dispatching a NodeUse h2@

The runtime exports two classifiers. @code isReservedNodeName code@ is true for
the 52 core-vocab names @strong plus strong@ @@@code node code@@@ (53 in all);
@code isCoreVocabName code@ is true for the core names @strong minus strong@
@@@code node code@@@. Use them to branch three ways:

@ul
@li @@@code use.name === RESERVED_OPAQUE code@@@ (the string @code 'node' code@) —
the opaque container. Dispatch on its @code type code@ param (see
@a |href /docs/build/custom-nodes/| Custom nodes a@). li@
@li @code isReservedNodeName(use.name) code@ — core vocab. Map it to your target's
element in a lookup table of your own. li@
@li Otherwise — a bound user definition. Its body was @strong already inlined strong@
by @code expand code@, so just emit @code use.body code@. li@
ul@

@h2 Reading params h2@

@code getParam(use, 'x') code@ reads a string value. A param that referenced data
carries @code param.typedValue code@ — a @code DataValue code@ — so read that
rather than re-parsing @code param.value code@:

@@@pre
const p = use.params.find((x: any) => x.name === 'rows');
const rows = p?.typedValue;   // a Collection, ready to walk — don't re-parse p.value
pre@@@

@h2 Every edge to cover h2@

@ul
@li @strong Raw and frozen bodies. strong@ When @code use.raw code@ or
@code use.frozen code@ is set, @code body code@ is a single verbatim
@code Text code@ child. Emit it unescaped @em only em@ for raw-text elements you
trust (@@@code @@style code@@@, @@@code @@script code@@@); escape everything
else. li@
@li @strong Inline vs. block. strong@ @code use.inline code@ tells you which;
mirror the HTML renderer's @code BLOCK_KINDS code@ set for the rest. li@
@li @strong A surviving access path. strong@ @code use.access code@ on a
@code NodeUse code@ that survived expansion is an unresolved data path — surface
it visibly rather than dropping it (the HTML renderer emits a
@code wit-unresolved code@ span). li@
@li @strong body can be null. strong@ Guard @@@code (use.body ?? []) code@@@; a
self-closing node has @code null code@, not @@@code [] code@@@. li@
@li @strong Testing. strong@ Mirror the ship renderers' test files — parse,
resolve, and expand a fixture, then assert on your output string. li@
ul@

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Handling @code if code@, @code each code@, or @code nodeDef code@ in your walk.
They are gone after @code expand code@; if you see one, expansion failed. li@
@li Re-parsing @code param.value code@ when @code param.typedValue code@ already
holds the structured value. li@
@li Escaping a raw @@@code @@style code@@@ or @@@code @@script code@@@ body. li@
@li Forgetting @code body code@ can be @code null code@. li@
ul@

